This research focuses on solving the given biharmonic Choquard type equation \begin{align*}
\begin{split}
\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
\Delta^2u=\left(\frac{f(u)}{u^\alpha}\right)^{1/(2-\alpha)}\quad\text{in}\quad\mathbb{R}^4,
\end{array}
\right.
\end{split} \end{align*} where $\alpha\in (0,2)$ and $f$ exhibits exponential growth, and establishes the existence of a standardized ground state solution when a certain parameter $\epsilon$ is small enough.

Recent analyses of lepton flavor universality (LFU) violating $b 	o c 	au 
u$ transitions have yielded both intriguing outcomes, with some deviations from predicted norms. To explore the potential connections between these events and the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ transition, we performed a comprehensive assessment within the context of the $U_1$ leptoquark model. By incorporating the impact of the former transitions on the latter, we discovered that, in order to align with observed data, the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay width could be significantly increased, with possible effects on the $U_1$ leptoquark's mass. Additionally, we found that, in the $b 	o c 	au -e$ extension of the $U_1$ model, the currently observed anomalies in $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ transitions could be partially mitigated, while the $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au  ar{
u}$ decay width remains largely unaffected. Nonetheless, the $U_1$ leptoquark's mass still suffers from significant uncertainty in this scenario.
